    fix(verify-node): refuse a non-digit --port and stop interpolating it into the remote shell (CF-270)
    
    `--port` was taken verbatim into PORT (line 25) and then expanded LOCALLY inside a
    double-quoted string that was handed to a REMOTE `bash -lc` at inside() (line 68), so a
    value carrying a quote closed the single quotes and ran on the node.
    
    Measured before (stub transport recording the argv, port `10800'; touch /tmp/cf270-canary; echo '`):
    
      exec --server stub node1 -- bash -lc curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 5 'http://127.0.0.1:10800'; touch /tmp/cf270-canary; echo '/api/status'
    
    `--port not-a-port` also ran the whole ladder and ended `VERIFY: UNVERIFIED` (exit 3)
    instead of being refused.
    
    After:
    
      exec --server fixture cf-fixture -- bash -lc curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" --max-time 5 "http://127.0.0.1:$1$2" _ 10800 /api/status
    
    The remote body is now a FIXED single-quoted script that only reads its own "$1"/"$2", and
    the port and path travel after `_` as POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS, so neither value can be
    re-parsed as shell syntax. PORT is validated immediately after the argument loop and before
    any use: it must match ^[0-9]+$ and be 1..65535, otherwise
    
      verify-node: --port must be digits 1-65535 (got: <value>)   -> stderr, exit 2
    
    (exit 2 is the code `unknown arg` and a missing --agent already use). A >5-digit value is
    refused before the base-10 expansion so the shell cannot print its own overflow error, and
    PORT is normalised (`10800` -> `10800`) before it reaches a message or a probe.
    
    Behaviour is otherwise unchanged: same legs, same PASS/FAIL/WARN/UNVERIFIED wording, same
    exit codes, same CF-224 transport distinction; the only lines that moved are the two inside()
    calls and the validation above them. The optional `--artifact` leg (line 134) is NOT
    rewritten: its value already crosses as shq-quoted data since CF-159, and section 36 pins
    that exact payload shape, so re-shaping it here would be a redesign of another row's arm.
    
    Tests — `tests/selftest.sh` section 48 (part of an existing numbered section, so the census
    is unchanged: `arms_plan=57 arms_tree=57`), five rows over a stub `bunker` that RECORDS the
    argv it is handed and never executes it:
    
      - `--port not-a-port` -> exit 2, stderr names --port, the record file is EMPTY, no VERIFY line
      - `--port 0 / 65536 / empty / ' 10800'` -> exit 2 each, record file EMPTY
      - the measured injection value -> exit 2, record file EMPTY, the canary file is never created
      - control `--port 10800` -> both inside() legs run, recorded as `"http://127.0.0.1:$1$2" _ 10800 <path>`, no canary in any command
      - pre-fix red control: the pre-fix inside() body, inlined, puts the canary INSIDE the recorded remote command
    
    Red/green, measured: the same arms re-run against the pre-fix `scripts/verify-node.sh`
    (HEAD 2f070b0) print `SELFTEST: FAIL (4)` with those four FAIL rows (exit 3, 426 recorded
    transport lines, 0 inside() calls in the fixed shape); on this tree all five rows PASS and
    `SELFTEST: PASS`, exit 0.
    
    Gates: `bash tests/selftest.sh` -> SELFTEST: PASS, rc 0 (389 PASS rows, 59 FAIL rows, all
    intentional negative fixtures); `bash -n scripts/verify-node.sh tests/selftest.sh` -> clean.
    
    Addresses CF-270.
